[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 968,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 733,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136967,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 733,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar osseous choristoma is a choristoma composed of mature bone.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 969,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 734,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136968,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136969,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LA14.06 Epibulbar choristoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136970,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> episcleral osseous choristoma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> osteoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136971,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136972,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar osseous choristomas are typically unilateral and located in the superotemporal conjunctiva {22496017;29589231;22516954;13871021}. They can also occur temporally, and rarely they occur in the inferotemporal conjunctiva {13871021}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136973,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar osseous choristoma presents as a yellow, orange, pink, or white subconjunctival mass that may be multinodular and can be freely mobile or adherent to the sclera or muscles. The mass usually extends from the equator to the superotemporal fornix. Epibulbar osseous choristomas are firm, and calcifications can be seen using CT {22496017}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136974,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The incidence of epibulbar osseous choristoma is unknown. It is present from birth and frequently presents in patients aged &lt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;years {22496017;12358295}, but it can also be discovered later in life {18569837}. There appears to be a slight female predilection {13871021;12358295}. Most of the reported cases have been in White people. However, a few cases have been reported in African-American patients {22516954;13871021}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136975,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar osseous choristoma is by definition a benign congenital lesion of histologically mature bone in an abnormal location.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136976,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136977,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Firm whitish-yellowish mass</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136978,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar osseous choristomas are composed of compact lamellar bone with normal osteocytes. Haversian canals can be found. Haematopoietic bone marrow is usually absent but has been reported rarely {12358295}. The bone is surrounded by periosteum and an internal osteogenic layer {13871021}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136979,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 980,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 745,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136980,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 745,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 983,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 748,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136981,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 748,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> compact mature lamellar bone with normal osteocytes present at an episcleral location.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 981,
